{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfbga6UsXR5Niiga6qXd5", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that **lists are mutable and tuples are immutable**. Most of the other differences follow from that.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutable | Yes: you can append, remove and reassign items | No: fixed once created |\n| Methods | Many (`append`, `extend`, `insert`, `pop`, `remove`, `sort`, `reverse`, \u2026) | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Usable as dict key / set member | No | Yes |\n| Memory / speed | Slightly larger, since it keeps spare room to grow | Smaller and a bit faster to create |\n| Typical use | A collection of similar items that may change | A fixed record where each position means something |\n\n### Examples\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums.append(4)        # OK -> [1, 2, 3, 4]\nnums[0] = 99          # OK\n\npoint = (3, 4)\npoint[0] = 10         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n\n# Tuples can be dict keys; lists can't\ndistances = {(0, 0): 0, (3, 4): 5}\n{[0, 0]: 0}           # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n```\n\n### Things that catch people out\n\n- **A single-element tuple needs a trailing comma:** `(5,)` is a tuple, but `(5)` is just the integer `5`.\n- **Tuples are only shallowly immutable.** You can't swap out the items in a tuple, but an item that is itself mutable can still change:\n  ```python\n  t = ([1, 2], 3)\n  t[0].append(99)     # works -> ([1, 2, 99], 3)\n  hash(t)             # TypeError, because it contains a list\n  ```\n- **Tuple packing and unpacking** happen all the time, often without you noticing: `x, y = y, x`, returning several values from a function, and `for k, v in d.items()`.\n\n### Which to use\n\n- Use a **list** when you have a variable-length group of similar items that you'll add to, remove from, or sort.\n- Use a **tuple** when you have a fixed group of values where position has meaning, like coordinates, RGB values or database rows. Also use one when you need something hashable or want to make it clear that the data shouldn't change.\n- For records with several fields, `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple` lets you access fields by name (`p.x`) and keeps all the benefits of a tuple."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7209, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16780, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7209}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "2c31d2a7-c3fb-4571-badc-1f8c0bce809c", "uuid": "34caf39d-c0bc-424f-9c48-ba83ebab2098", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T14:34:44.578Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfbga67YLoKgL9qkU758A"}
